<title>USA: U.S. Q1 non-farm productivity rev to up 2.6 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q1 non-farm productivity rev to up 2.6 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor estimates of U.S.</p>
<p>productivity/costs, seasonally adjusted annual percent changes</p>
<p>from prior quarters, on chain weights basis (1992=100):</p>
<p>    -	     - Productivity -	  - - -Output- - -</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4	  Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.6   2.0   1.3	   7.0   6.8   4.9</p>
<p> Manufacturing   2.8   3.1   3.7	   5.6   6.7   4.3</p>
<p> Durable Goods   3.3   3.4   2.1	   8.6  10.3   2.8</p>
<p> NonDurables     3.1   3.5   5.5	   2.5   2.6   5.9</p>
<p> Total Business  2.9   2.1   1.3	   7.0   6.8   4.8</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>    -	     - - -Hours- - -	Hourly Compensation</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4	  Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   4.2   4.7   3.6	   5.2   4.7   3.7</p>
<p> Manufacturing   2.8   3.5   0.6	   4.4   4.5   2.6</p>
<p> Durable Goods   5.2   6.6   0.7	   4.8   4.4   1.4</p>
<p> NonDurables    -0.6  -0.8   0.4	   3.2   4.0   4.6</p>
<p> Total Business  4.0   4.5   3.4	   5.0   4.5   3.7</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.  </p>
<p>    -	 Real Hourly Compensation    Unit Labor Costs</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4	  Q1-R   Q1-P   Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.8   2.3   0.3	   2.5   2.7   2.4</p>
<p> Manufacturing   2.0   2.0  -0.7	   1.6   1.4  -1.1</p>
<p> Durable Goods   2.4   2.0  -1.9	   1.5   0.9  -0.7</p>
<p> NonDurables     0.8   1.6   1.2	   0.1   0.5  -0.8</p>
<p> Total Business  2.6   2.0   0.3	   2.1   2.3   2.3</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast Q1 non-farm productivity to be revised to a 2.1 percent increase and unit labor costs to be revised to up 2.6 percent.</p>
<p>Compared to the first quarter of 1996, first quarter 1997 non-farm productivity rose 1.0 percent, output rose 4.6 percent, hours rose 3.6 percent, hourly compensation increased 4.0 percent, real hourly compensation rose 1.0 percent and unit labor costs increased 3.0 percent.</p>
<p>The department said the revised first quarter gain of 2.6 percent in non-farm productivity was the largest since a 2.6 percent increase in the fourth quarter of 1993.</p>
<p>It was last exceeded by a 5.9 percent increase in the fourth quarter of 1992.</p>
